{"id":10411,"date":"2022-01-10T12:21:20","date_gmt":"2022-01-10T12:21:20","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/nicoletcz.cz\/solutions\/science-research-education\/nanoscale-phase-transitions\/"},"modified":"2022-01-10T12:21:22","modified_gmt":"2022-01-10T12:21:22","slug":"nanoscale-phase-transitions","status":"publish","type":"page","link":"https:\/\/nicoletcz.cz\/en\/solutions\/science-research-education\/nanoscale-phase-transitions\/","title":{"rendered":"Nanoscale phase transitions"},"content":{"rendered":"\t\t<div data-elementor-type=\"wp-page\" data-elementor-id=\"10411\" class=\"elementor elementor-10411 elementor-9926\" data-elementor-post-type=\"page\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<section class=\"elementor-section elementor-top-section elementor-element elementor-element-7b6412e elementor-section-boxed elementor-section-height-default elementor-section-height-default\" data-id=\"7b6412e\" data-element_type=\"section\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"elementor-container elementor-column-gap-default\">\n\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"elementor-column elementor-col-100 elementor-top-column elementor-element elementor-element-0af7ddf\" data-id=\"0af7ddf\" data-element_type=\"column\">\n\t\t\t<div class=\"elementor-widget-wrap elementor-element-populated\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"elementor-element elementor-element-021f2ce elementor-widget elementor-widget-eael-adv-tabs\" data-id=\"021f2ce\" data-element_type=\"widget\" data-widget_type=\"eael-adv-tabs.default\">\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"elementor-widget-container\">\n\t\t\t\t\t        <div data-scroll-on-click=\"no\" data-scroll-speed=\"300\" id=\"eael-advance-tabs-021f2ce\" class=\"eael-advance-tabs eael-tabs-horizontal eael-tab-auto-active  active-caret-on responsive-vertical-layout\" data-tabid=\"021f2ce\">\n            <div class=\"eael-tabs-nav\">\n                <ul class=\"\" role=\"tablist\">\n                                            <li id=\"application-description\" class=\"inactive eael-tab-item-trigger eael-tab-nav-item\" aria-selected=\"true\" data-tab=\"1\" role=\"tab\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-controls=\"application-description-tab\" aria-expanded=\"false\">\n                            \n                            \n                            \n                                                            <span class=\"eael-tab-title title-after-icon\" >Application description<\/span>                                                    <\/li>\n                                            <li id=\"neascope-product-line\" class=\"inactive eael-tab-item-trigger eael-tab-nav-item\" aria-selected=\"false\" data-tab=\"2\" role=\"tab\" tabindex=\"-1\" aria-controls=\"neascope-product-line-tab\" aria-expanded=\"false\">\n                            \n                            \n                            \n                                                            <span class=\"eael-tab-title title-after-icon\" >neaSCOPE product line<\/span>                                                    <\/li>\n                                    <\/ul>\n            <\/div>\n            \n            <div class=\"eael-tabs-content\">\n\t\t        \n                    <div id=\"application-description-tab\" class=\"clearfix eael-tab-content-item inactive\" data-title-link=\"application-description-tab\">\n\t\t\t\t        <p>The high spatial resolution of near-field infrared microscopy allows the <a href=\"https:\/\/www.neaspec.com\/products\/neaspec-microscopes\/\"><strong>neaSCOPE systems<\/strong><\/a> to study in detail the phase transitions in materials, such as the transition of an insulator to a metal in thin layers of vanadium dioxide (VO<sub>2<\/sub>).<\/p>\n<figure id=\"attachment_9927\" aria-describedby=\"caption-attachment-9927\" style=\"width: 800px\" class=\"wp-caption aligncenter\"><img fetchpriority=\"high\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"wp-image-9927 size-large\" src=\"https:\/\/nicoletcz.cz\/app\/uploads\/2021\/12\/df641878-1024x546.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"800\" height=\"427\" srcset=\"https:\/\/nicoletcz.cz\/app\/uploads\/2021\/12\/df641878-1024x546.jpg 1024w, https:\/\/nicoletcz.cz\/app\/uploads\/2021\/12\/df641878-300x160.jpg 300w, https:\/\/nicoletcz.cz\/app\/uploads\/2021\/12\/df641878-768x410.jpg 768w, https:\/\/nicoletcz.cz\/app\/uploads\/2021\/12\/df641878.jpg 1160w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 800px) 100vw, 800px\" \/><figcaption id=\"caption-attachment-9927\" class=\"wp-caption-text\">Analysis of temperature induced vanadium dioxide phase transition<\/figcaption><\/figure>\n<p>Near-field infrared microscopy provides a new perspective on the temperature-induced VO<sub>2<\/sub> phase transition. The measured data directly confirm the percolative nature of the VO<sub>2<\/sub> thin film phase transition, where the insulating and metal phases coexist in the temperature range in which the transition occurs. Data measured for a wavelength of 10.75 \u00b5m with a controlled temperature change between 340 K and 343 K reveal the formation of metal &#8220;nano-pools&#8221;, which show a higher signal amplitude for areas of metal samples compared to insulator.<\/p>\n<p>Maps measured for VO<sub>2<\/sub> thin film phase transitions show that near-field IR microscopy allows detailed phase transition studies in nanomaterials. The technique can be applied not only to phase transitions related to VO<sub>2<\/sub>, but also to <strong>all other phase changes<\/strong> during which a dielectric\/metallic or amorphous\/crystalline transition occurs (as with GeSbTe alloys).<\/p>\n<p>You can learn about <a href=\"https:\/\/www.neaspec.com\/application\/e-book-nanoscale-analysis-of-inorganic-materials\/\">other applications here<\/a>.<\/p>\n                    <\/div>\n\t\t        \n                    <div id=\"neascope-product-line-tab\" class=\"clearfix eael-tab-content-item inactive\" data-title-link=\"neascope-product-line-tab\">\n\t\t\t\t        <style>@media(max-width:1024px){.elementor-6756 .elementor-element.elementor-element-43f4b2a9 .dce-post-item{padding:0px 0px 20p